#!/usr/bin/env bash
trap 'killm f' EXIT
#
# bpsec-all-multinode-test.bsl
#
# BSL (Bundle Security Library) multinode BPSec integration test.
# Verifies BIB (integrity) and BCB (confidentiality) security processing
# across a 3-node topology using LTP convergence layer.
#
# Topology:
#   Node 2 (ipn:2.*) --LTP--> Node 3 (ipn:3.*) --LTP--> Node 4 (ipn:4.*)
#
# Security design (RFC 9172 compliant):
#   Per RFC 9172 Section 4.7, a single security source MUST NOT apply
#   separate BIB and BCB to the same target block. Instead:
#     - BIB targets the PRIMARY block (tgt 0) for integrity
#     - BCB targets the PAYLOAD block (tgt 1) for confidentiality
#   This ensures each security service operates on a different block.
#
# BSL policy is loaded at startup via "m bsl" in each node's bprc.
#   Node 2 (source):
#     - BIB source on primary (tgt 0) for 2->3 and 2->4
#     - BCB source on payload (tgt 1) for 2->3 and 2->4
#     - BIB source on the bundle age block (tgt 7) and the previous node
#       block (tgt 6), both of which ION does not fill in until the
#       bundle is dequeued for transmission.  These two rules must be
#       DECLINED: there is no settled content to protect while the
#       bundle is at rest, and ION would overwrite it at dequeue.  They
#       are a regression guard - offering such a block to BSL as a
#       zero-length target used to crash the sending process, and it
#       took two of them to do it, since the first zero-length target
#       left BSL's stack copy of the BTSD null and only the second
#       reached it as a garbage pointer.  Every test below must still
#       pass with these rules in place.
#   Node 3 (waypoint/dest for 2->3, relay for 2->4):
#     - BCB verifier at CLIN (validates ASB, does NOT decrypt)
#     - BIB acceptor at APPOUT for primary (verifies HMAC)
#     - BCB acceptor at APPOUT for payload (decrypts)
#     - BCB relay verifier at CLIN for 2->4 transit traffic
#     - BIB source at CLOUT on primary for 2->4 transit traffic.
#       CLOUT is the second location at which BSL can source a
#       security block, and the only one exercised after the
#       bundle already carries security blocks of its own.  A
#       block sourced here must be CBOR-encoded and counted in
#       the bundle's extensions length before serialization,
#       while the blocks node 2 already added must be left
#       alone.  Tests 6 and 8 fail if either half regresses.
#     - BIB source at CLOUT on the bundle age block (tgt 7), the
#       previous node block (tgt 6) and the metadata block (tgt 8).
#       These are the same kind of block that node 2 must decline at
#       APPIN, protected at the one location where they can be: by
#       CLOUT the content ION writes at dequeue is settled, so there
#       is something to sign.  See the note on scope below.
#   Node 4 (dest for 2->4):
#     - BCB verifier at CLIN (validates ASB, does NOT decrypt)
#     - BIB acceptor at APPOUT for primary (verifies HMAC)
#     - BCB acceptor at APPOUT for payload (decrypts)
#     - BIB acceptor at APPOUT for the three blocks node 3 protected
#       at CLOUT
#
# Scope of a security operation, and why the location matters:
#   A BIB sourced when the bundle is created protects the block from
#   the source to the destination, and so requires that no node in
#   between rewrite it.  A BIB sourced while the bundle is forwarded
#   protects it for exactly one hop, and must be accepted by the next
#   node rather than carried further.
#
#   The bundle age and previous node blocks are rewritten at every
#   dequeue, so only the second form can ever apply to them: an
#   end-to-end signature over content the next hop replaces could not
#   verify.  The metadata block is not rewritten in transit, but ION
#   still does not serialize it until dequeue, so it too has nothing
#   to protect at APPIN.  That is why node 3 sources over all three and
#   node 4 accepts all three.
#
# Note: BIB verifiers are NOT used at CLIN because BIB on the primary
# block does not depend on BCB processing order (BIB targets a different
# block than BCB). However, BIB verification at CLIN is unnecessary for
# this test since the acceptor at APPOUT performs the same check. BCB
# verifiers at CLIN perform a lightweight ASB validation without decrypting.
#
# Tests:
#   Test 1 - Small bundle node 2->3 with BIB(primary)+BCB(payload)
#   Test 2 - Small bundle node 2->3, verify BSL initialization
#   Test 3 - Wrong-key rejection (node 3 restarts with wrong keys, bundle rejected)
#   Test 4 - Small bundle node 2->3 (baseline without dynamic rule changes)
#   Test 5 - Large ~4MB file node 2->3 with BIB(primary)+BCB(payload)
#   Test 6 - Small bundle node 2->4 via node 3 relay, BCB relay verification
#   Test 7 - Anonymous bundle node 2->4 via node 3 relay
#   Test 8 - Small bundle node 2->4 via node 3 relay (exercises SDR BTSD
#            read path for BIB on primary at acceptor)
#
# Originally based on test by Scott Burleigh, April 12, 2010.
# Adapted to BSP by Ryan Brown, August 2011.
# Adapted to BSL multinode test with per-node policy files.

TIMETODISPLAYTEXT=2
